  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 AM EDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nCurrent water vapor satellite imagery shows two prominent, and \nnecessary, features of note to our west. First is an upper low\nin the vicinity of the front range, another is a trough digging\nacross Canada and the adjacent Dakotas. Relatively weak sfc\nreflections associated with both of these features, although the\nlow pressure system associated with the upper low near the front\nrange will inject an anomalous amount of moisture northward into\nnorthern Michigan later today and tonight as it tracks northeast\nacross southwest Michigan.\n\nWarm, moist advection in conjunction with synoptic forcing will\noverspread the region beginning this afternoon, and remaining \ninto portions of the overnight hours. Steady moderate rain is \nexpected as a result, spreading from southwest to northeast on \nthe north and northwest of the surface low track. Bit of a lull \novernight due to the sfc low track for some. As the surface low\npulls off to the northeast through the day on Friday, winds \nshift to the NNW with showers continuing. Cold advection and \ncrashing H850 temps will result in lake convection as well, and \nthus through the day the typical NNW lake effect zones will \nbecome favored with continued showers. When all is said and \ndone, the latest guidance shows a healthy swath of ~0.60-1.0\"+ \nof rain, with local highs and lows, possible by Friday evening. \nPressure gradient tightens as the sfc low moves off to the east\nwith increasing low level winds. Wind gusts look to be in the \n25 to 40 mph range, highest near Lake Michigan and during the\nlate afternoon and evening. Moisture, synoptic ascent, and the\npressure gradient relinquish through the night on Friday, and \nthus, precipitation chances, and winds, wane through the night.\n\nSignificant troughing moving west to east across Canada this\nweekend will result in deepening low pressure system across\nManitoba, Saskatchewan, and up into the vicinity of Hudson Bay.\nAt the same time, sfc high pressure will reside and slowly shift\nacross the Ohio Valley and Northeast. In-between these two\nsurface features (N MI) will be a strengthening pressure \ngradient and thus breezy to windy conditions. Windiest day will\nbe Sunday. It will still be cool on Saturday but by Sunday,\nthose southwest winds will usher in a milder mass, with 60s \nexpected. \n\nHeights aloft remain above normal through the early to middle\nportions of next week. Consequently, high temperatures remain\nwell above normal for this time of year, with 60s common. The\npattern will also be conducive to dry conditions.",
